    <h1>Forest Raven vs Australian Raven: How to Tell Them Apart</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The single most reliable field mark is the call: the Australian Raven gives a prolonged, wailing last note; the Forest Raven gives a shorter, deeper "korr." At rest, the Forest Raven has a comparatively shorter, rounder throat hackle and a stubbier bill. In Tasmania, where both species occur, voice is the go-to separation.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Side-by-Side Comparison</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Forest Raven</th><th>Australian Raven</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Scientific name</td><td><em>Corvus tasmanicus</em></td><td><em>Corvus coronoides</em></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Body length</td><td>50–53 cm</td><td>52–53 cm (slightly larger)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Call</td><td>Short, deep "korr-korr-korr"</td><td>Prolonged wailing "aah-aah-aaaah" (last note drawn out)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Throat hackle</td><td>Shorter, rounder; not strongly pointed</td><td>Long, pointed; often held erect</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bill</td><td>Shorter, less deeply curved</td><td>Heavier; more deeply arched</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Eye colour</td><td>White (adult)</td><td>White (adult)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Habitat</td><td>Dense eucalypt forest, highland areas, Tasmania</td><td>Open woodland, farmland, suburbs — wider range</td></tr>
