        <tr><td>IUCN status</td><td>Least Concern</td><td>Least Concern</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Call — The Key to Separation</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Both species are large, all-black corvids with white eyes, making visual separation difficult, particularly in flight. The call is the most reliable character and can be used at distance.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p><strong>Australian Raven:</strong> The characteristic call is a wailing "aaah-aaah-aaaah," with the final syllable drawn out and descending in pitch — often described as mournful or "complaining." This wail is unique among Australian corvids and immediately separates it from the Forest Raven.</p>
    <p><strong>Forest Raven:</strong> The call is a deep, short "korr" or "korr-korr-korr" — powerful and resonant, but lacking the drawn-out wail. It is similar in quality to a Common Raven call, reflecting the Forest Raven's status as the most "raven-like" of the Australian corvids.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Plumage and Structure</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Both species are entirely glossy black with a blue-green and purple iridescence. Both have white irises as adults. Structural differences are subtle but useful when good views are obtained:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Throat hackle:</strong> the Australian Raven's hackle is notably long and pointed, often visible as a "beard" when held erect. The Forest Raven's hackle is shorter and less pointed.</li>
      <li><strong>Bill:</strong> both have heavy corvid bills, but the Australian Raven's tends to be deeper and more strongly arched — a character easier to see in direct comparison than in isolation.</li>
      <li><strong>Overall build:</strong> the Australian Raven tends to look heavier-bodied and longer-necked; the Forest Raven more compact.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Where They Overlap</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Forest Raven is primarily a Tasmanian species, with small populations in coastal Victoria (around Wilson's Promontory) and northeast New South Wales. The Australian Raven is widespread across southeast Australia and also occurs in Tasmania. In Tasmania, the two species share the landscape but prefer different habitats: Forest Ravens favour dense, tall eucalypt forest and highland areas; Australian Ravens prefer more open woodland, farmland, and urban edges.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

